# yt-dlp and ffmpeg for the macOS and Windows packages, pinned by digest,
# which Patina finds and runs by itself (src/platform.rs, `bundled`): yt-dlp
# for online video and the YouTube section, ffmpeg for downloads that join
# separate video and audio, which without it come as the best single file,
# often 360p. The Flatpak gets its own from its manifest and the runtime, and
# a Linux build uses the system's. yt-dlp is read by packaging/macos-tools.sh,
# which release.yml's macos job runs, and by packaging/windows-deps.ps1
# -Tools in its windows job, neither of which caches the downloads; ffmpeg's
# source by packaging/ffmpeg.sh, which builds it in the macos and
# ffmpeg-windows jobs.
#
# yt-dlp: its own standalone builds, from the release's assets. yt-dlp_macos
# is universal (x86_64 for macOS 10.13 and later, arm64 for 11 and later)
# and yt-dlp.exe is x86_64 for Windows; both are PyInstaller programs that
# unpack themselves on every start. Both carry yt-dlp-ejs but no JavaScript
# runtime, and none is bundled: without one yt-dlp asks YouTube's `visionos`
# client, which needs none, and Patina asks for that client first anyway
# (settings::ytdl_youtube_clients).
#
# yt-dlp's own code is Unlicense, but these builds are not: they carry
# mutagen (GPL-2.0-or-later) and certifi (MPL-2.0) among the rest, so each
# is GPL-3.0-or-later as a combined work, as yt-dlp's README says under
# "Licensing". So the GPLv3 text (GPLV3_LICENSE below) goes beside them in
# both packages, and THIRD-PARTY.md says where the source is: yt-dlp's tag,
# whose bundle/requirements/ name the exact version of everything the build
# carries and whose .github/workflows/build.yml and bundle/pyinstaller.py
# built it. The notices for what the builds carry (Python, OpenSSL, mutagen,
# certifi and the rest) are THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt from the release's tag,
# which the release assets leave out.
#
# yt-dlp has to stay current, since YouTube changes and fixes come in new
# releases, and Patina doesn't update it by itself; the YouTube section says
# when the copy is more than 90 days old. So refresh these pins for every
# Patina release, with the Flatpak manifest's (release.yml's version job
# fails a tag whose commit pins one older than the newest there was), and
# make a release for a yt-dlp fix that matters. yt-dlp's releases are
# immutable and attested, which release.yml checks; to refresh, print the
# newest one's tag and digests:
#   gh api repos/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/latest --jq '.tag_name, (.assets[] | select(.name | test("^(yt-dlp_macos|yt-dlp[.]exe)$")) | "\(.browser_download_url) \(.digest | ltrimstr("sha256:"))")'
# then download both and check them against the release's signed
# SHA2-256SUMS (the key yt-dlp's README links to, public.key in its
# repository, was AC0CBBE6848D6A873464AF4E57CF65933B5A7581 for 2026.10.26),
# and with
#   gh release verify-asset <tag> <file> --repo yt-dlp/yt-dlp
# fetch THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt from raw.githubusercontent.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/<tag>/
# and hash it, and change the tag in every URL below together. Check too
# that the "Licensing" section of yt-dlp's README at the new tag still says
# what THIRD-PARTY.md does.
YTDLP_VERSION=2026.10.26
YTDLP_MACOS_URL=https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/download/2026.10.26/yt-dlp_macos
YTDLP_MACOS_SHA256=0f922c15ccc0b83eefa1c8bc9104e434318c4bf16e408e1ee93b7745a6efe0aa
YTDLP_WINDOWS_URL=https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/download/2026.10.26/yt-dlp.exe
YTDLP_WINDOWS_SHA256=f5389d3698ecde4ef06f4e6b1ce0294fe9ac1396228c8990b8d3416d6d177a7d
YTDLP_LICENSES_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/2026.10.26/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt
YTDLP_LICENSES_SHA256=472aefe951c7db35e1657c1d13fd337140511ed6f2b329205105ad441c5a02b7

# ffmpeg, for both packages: built in release.yml from FFmpeg's own source
# release by packaging/ffmpeg.sh, which holds the configuration and says why
# each part of it is there, rather than taken from someone's prebuilt
# binaries. Those are made to do everything, 130 MB and more each, and
# those for Apple silicon are built for macOS 12; this only joins video and
# audio and makes yt-dlp's fixes by copying streams, a few MB, for macOS 11
# as the rest of Patina.app is. LGPL 2.1 or later as configured, and its
# corresponding source is this tarball and that script, which
# THIRD-PARTY.md names. Built afresh by every release run, like the rest
# of the packages.
#
# The newest stable release, from https://ffmpeg.org/download.html. To
# refresh, download ffmpeg-<version>.tar.xz and its .asc from
# https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ and FFmpeg's release key,
# https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-devel.asc, and check the signature in a keyring
# of its own:
#   export GNUPGHOME=$(mktemp -d)
#   gpg --import ffmpeg-devel.asc
#   gpg --verify ffmpeg-<version>.tar.xz.asc ffmpeg-<version>.tar.xz
# which has to name the key FCF986EA15E6E293A5644F10B4322F04D67658D8
# ("FFmpeg release signing key", as for 9.0.2); then change the version,
# URL and digest together, with the version and digest THIRD-PARTY.md names
# (release.yml checks the digest there), and check that configure, built
# with `packaging/ffmpeg.sh linux`, still says "License: LGPL version 2.1 or
# later" and lists the components the script asks for, and that the joins
# and fixes the script's comment lists still work through yt-dlp.
FFMPEG_VERSION=9.0.2
FFMPEG_SOURCE_URL=https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz
FFMPEG_SOURCE_SHA256=8c3850283eb25fa026482078a04051e0be17347b09ef81a0849bec15a96e002e

# The GPLv3 text, as LICENSE-GPLv3.txt beside both packages' yt-dlp, which
# is under it. FFmpeg's copy, from the tag of the version above; the text is
# the FSF's and doesn't change between tags, so this pin needn't move with
# FFmpeg's.
GPLV3_LICENSE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg/n9.0.2/COPYING.GPLv3
GPLV3_LICENSE_SHA256=8ceb4b9ee5adedde47b31e975c1d90c73ad27b6b165a1dcd80c7c545eb65b903
